@page "/docs/installation"

<DocsPage Title="Installation" Description="How to install dependencies and structure your app.">
    <p>Choose the setup that matches your starting point.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:gap-6">
        <a href="#new-project" class="flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1 rounded-2xl bg-(--surface) p-6 text-sm text-(--surface-foreground) no-underline! transition-colors hover:bg-(--surface)/80">
            <div class="font-medium">New Project</div>
            <div class="leading-relaxed font-normal text-muted-foreground">Create a Blazor app and set it up with the CLI.</div>
        </a>
        <a href="#existing-project" class="flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1 rounded-2xl bg-(--surface) p-6 text-sm text-(--surface-foreground) no-underline! transition-colors hover:bg-(--surface)/80">
            <div class="font-medium">Existing Project</div>
            <div class="leading-relaxed font-normal text-muted-foreground">Add Blazix to an app you already created.</div>
        </a>
    </div>

    <DocsHeading Title="New Project" />
    <p>You need the .NET 10 SDK. Nothing else: no Node.js, npm or <code>package.json</code>.</p>
    <Steps>
        <Step>Create a Blazor app</Step>
        <p>Components need interactivity. Create a Blazor Web App with interactive server rendering:</p>
        <CliCommandRaw Command="dotnet new blazor -n MyApp --interactivity Server --all-interactive" />
        <Step>Run the init command</Step>
        <p>From the app&apos;s folder, run <code>init</code> to set up Tailwind, the theme and the usings:</p>
        <CliCommand Args="init" />
        <Step>Add components</Step>
        <p>You can now start adding components to your project.</p>
        <CliCommand Args="add button" />
        <p>The command above adds the <code>Button</code> component to <code>Components/UI/Button</code>. You can then use it in a page:</p>
        <CodeFigure Code="@PageCode" Title="Components/Pages/Home.razor" LineNumbers="true" />
        <Step>Run your app</Step>
        <CliCommandRaw Command="dotnet watch" />
        <p>Tailwind builds with your project, and recompiles as you edit while <code>dotnet watch</code> runs.</p>
    </Steps>

    <DocsHeading Title="Existing Project" />
    <p>Run <code>init</code> in the folder of your Blazor app (or pass <code>--project</code>). It changes your project like this, and every change is a plain file you can read and edit:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Adds <code>Tailwind.targets</code> and imports it in your <code>.csproj</code>. Tailwind now builds with your project, using the standalone Tailwind CLI, which is downloaded on the first build.</li>
        <li>Adds <code>TailwindWatcher.cs</code> and one line in <code>Program.cs</code>. In development, Tailwind recompiles as you edit, so hot reload shows new classes.</li>
        <li>Moves your existing <code>wwwroot/app.css</code> to <code>Styles/site.css</code> and imports it, so none of your styles are lost.</li>
        <li>Adds <code>Styles/theme.css</code>: the design tokens. See <a href="/docs/theming">Theming</a>.</li>
        <li>Adds <code>Styles/Vendor/tw-animate.css</code> and <code>Styles/Vendor/variants.css</code>, the animation utilities and variants the components use.</li>
        <li>Adds <code>@@using</code> lines for your components to <code>_Imports.razor</code>, and a global using for <code>Blazix.Primitives</code> to your <code>.csproj</code>.</li>
        <li>Adds the <code>Blazix.Primitives</code> package, at the same version as the CLI.</li>
        <li>Writes <a href="/docs/blazix-json"><code>blazix.json</code></a>, which records your namespace, components folder and what you&apos;ve installed.</li>
    </ul>
    <CliCommand Args="init" />
    <p>Then add components with <code>add</code>, as above.</p>
    <p>
        If your app has a <code>.Client</code> project (the WebAssembly and Auto render modes), run <code>init</code> in either project,
        or in the solution folder above them. The components, usings, package and <code>blazix.json</code> go in the <code>.Client</code>
        project, so pages in every render mode can use them. Tailwind and <code>TailwindWatcher.cs</code> go in the server project, which
        serves the CSS and scans the <code>.Client</code> project&apos;s files with <code>@@source</code>. The server project gets the usings too,
        for its layouts and static pages. <code>add</code> and <code>list</code> also work from any of these folders. See
        <a href="#render-modes">Render Modes</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        A standalone WebAssembly app has no server to run the watcher, so <code>init</code> leaves it out and links <code>app.css</code> in <code>wwwroot/index.html</code>.
        The CSS is recompiled on each build.
    </p>
    <Callout>
        <code>init</code> asks for Inter and falls back to the system font. To get Inter, load it in <code>App.razor</code>&apos;s <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. The comment at the top of <code>Styles/theme.css</code> shows how.
    </Callout>

    <DocsHeading Title="Render Modes" />
    <p>
        The components work in Interactive Server, Interactive WebAssembly and Interactive Auto apps, prerendered or not.
        In static server rendering, they render but don&apos;t respond: menus, dialogs and the rest need an interactive render mode.
    </p>
    <p>
        Interactive Auto runs a page on the server the first time, while the WebAssembly runtime downloads, and in the browser once
        it&apos;s cached, usually from the next full page load. Nothing changes in how you use the components. To set up an Auto app:
    </p>
    <CliCommandRaw Command="dotnet new blazor -n MyApp --interactivity Auto" />
    <CliCommand Args="init" />
    <p>Run <code>init</code> in the <code>MyApp</code> folder the template made. Then keep these in mind:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Components that use Blazix go in the <code>.Client</code> project</strong>, where <code>add</code> puts the components.
            The server can run them, but WebAssembly can only load what&apos;s in the client.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Give them a render mode.</strong> Without <code>--all-interactive</code>, the layout in the server project renders
            statically, so put <code>@@rendermode InteractiveAuto</code> on each page, or render an interactive part of the layout (a header
            with a menu) as its own component: <code>&lt;Header @@rendermode="InteractiveAuto" /&gt;</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Register services in both <code>Program.cs</code> files.</strong> A page runs on the server (prerendering, and Auto
            before WebAssembly) and in the browser, so a service it injects, like the <a href="/docs/components/toast"><code>ToastManager</code></a>,
            must be registered in both. Otherwise the page fails only once it runs in the browser. <code>add</code> tells you when one is missing.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cascading values stop at a render mode.</strong> A <code>DirectionProvider</code> or <code>Toaster</code> in a static
            layout doesn&apos;t reach an interactive page. Put the <code>Toaster</code> in an interactive part of the layout
            (<code>&lt;Toaster @@rendermode="InteractiveAuto" /&gt;</code>) or in the page. For right-to-left, cascade the direction
            from both <code>Program.cs</code> files instead: <code>builder.Services.AddCascadingValue(_ =&gt; TextDirection.Rtl);</code>.
            With <code>--all-interactive</code> the layout is interactive, and both work there as usual.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Parts of a page can run on different runtimes, for example a header on the server over a page on WebAssembly. That works:
        Escape, outside presses and modal dialogs see popups from both runtimes, and the ids they render can&apos;t collide. But each
        runtime has its own services, so a toast added on one runtime doesn&apos;t show in a <code>Toaster</code> on the other.
        Interactive Auto keeps the parts of a page on one runtime, so this only comes up when you mix fixed render modes.
    </p>
    <Callout>
        On the server, a modal dialog makes the rest of the page inert, but not Blazor&apos;s reconnect dialog or error bar. If the
        connection drops while a dialog is open, they can still be read and used.
    </Callout>

    <DocsHeading Title="Icons" />
    <p>
        The components draw their icons with the <code>Icon</code> component from <code>Blazix.Icons</code>. <code>add</code>
        installs the package of the set they use: <a href="https://lucide.dev" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Lucide</a>
        (<code>Blazix.Icons.Lucide</code>) unless you pick another set with <code>init --icon-library</code>. For icons of your
        own, add the set&apos;s package too. See <a href="/docs/icons">Icons</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The examples in these docs use <code>Blazix.Icons.Lucide</code> (<code>&lt;Icon Data="LucideIcons.ChevronRight" /&gt;</code>). To
        copy them as they are, add the package and <code>@@using Blazix.Icons</code> and <code>@@using Blazix.Icons.Lucide</code> to
        <code>_Imports.razor</code>.
    </p>
</DocsPage>

@code {
    private const string PageCode = """
        @page "/"

        <Button>Click me</Button>
        """;
}
